---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Detection Overview', href: '/docs/detection' },
  { title: 'Detection Capabilities', href: '/docs/detection/capabilities' },
  { title: 'Seeded CVEs', href: '/docs/detection/cves', active: true },
  { title: 'Suricata Network IDS', href: '/docs/detection/suricata' },
  { title: 'Pattern Database', href: '/docs/detection/patterns' },
  { title: 'Advanced Signals', href: '/docs/detection/advanced' },
  { title: 'Device & GDPR Signature', href: '/docs/detection/signature' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'direct', text: 'Direct CVE Matches', level: 2 },
  { id: 'suricata', text: 'Suricata Alert-Based', level: 2 },
  { id: 'abuseipdb', text: 'AbuseIPDB Mapping', level: 2 },
  { id: 'adding', text: 'Adding a CVE', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Seeded CVEs" description="The specific CVEs seeded into the honeypot to attract adversaries" section="Detection" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Two CVE paths exist. <strong>Direct matches</strong> are explicit patterns evaluated in Lua before the
    request is routed, so they can force a honeypot diversion on their own. <strong>Suricata alerts</strong>
    contribute severity-graded deltas from observed network traffic. Both paths feed the same risk score and
    the same AbuseIPDB reporting decision.
  </p>

  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="13 direct CVEs" icon="shieldAlert" tag="Lua patterns" description="WordPress plugin exploits matched against URI, arguments and required headers." meta="+40 per match" />
    <DocCard title="Suricata evidence" icon="network" tag="Network layer" description="Severity 1 adds +50, severity 2 adds +25, everything else adds +10." meta="capped at 100" />
    <DocCard title="Report enrichment" icon="clipboard" tag="AbuseIPDB" description="Matched CVE names are appended to the report comment for the analyst on the other side." meta="6 reason types" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="direct">Direct CVE Matches (via vulnerability_rules.lua)</h2>
  <p>The following CVEs are explicitly matched via URI/argument pattern analysis in the vulnerability rules. vulnerability_rules.detect_vulnerability_pattern() returns an object with cve, severity, description, plugin, attack_vector for each matched pattern.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>CVE ID</th><th>Severity</th><th>Plugin</th><th>Attack Vector</th><th>Match Condition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2023-28121</td><td>HIGH</td><td>woocommerce-payments</td><td>header_manipulation</td><td>URI contains wp-json/wp/v2/users AND X-WCPAY-PLATFORM-CHECKOUT-USER header</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2023-2986</td><td>HIGH</td><td>abandoned-cart-lite</td><td>encryption_bypass</td><td>URI/args contain wcal_action=checkout_link AND validate=</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2025-4403</td><td>CRITICAL</td><td>drag-and-drop-multiple-file-upload</td><td>file_upload_bypass</td><td>URI/args contain dnd_codedropz_upload AND supported_type</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2025-2266</td><td>CRITICAL</td><td>cwmp</td><td>privilege_escalation</td><td>URI/args contain cwmpupdateoptions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2025-47577</td><td>CRITICAL</td><td>gift-voucher</td><td>file_upload_rce</td><td>URI/args contain mwb_wgm_preview_mail</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2024-2387</td><td>HIGH</td><td>advanced-form-integration</td><td>sql_injection</td><td>URI contains advanced-form-integration-log AND args contain integration_id with quote/union/select</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2025-10142</td><td>HIGH</td><td>pagseguro-connect-woocommerce</td><td>path_traversal</td><td>URI/args contain pc_added_uploaded_image AND file[path]</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE-2024-50508</td><td>HIGH</td><td>wp-file-upload</td><td>directory_traversal</td><td>URI/args contain file[path] AND (../ or /etc/)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GENERIC-WP-001</td><td>CRITICAL</td><td>wordpress-core</td><td>sensitive_file_access</td><td>URI contains wp-config.php</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GENERIC-WP-002</td><td>HIGH</td><td>wordpress-core</td><td>installation_abuse</td><td>URI contains wp-admin/install.php or setup-config.php</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GENERIC-WP-003</td><td>MEDIUM</td><td>wordpress-core</td><td>information_disclosure</td><td>URI contains wp-json/wp/v2/users or author=digits</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GENERIC-WP-004</td><td>MEDIUM</td><td>wordpress-core</td><td>xmlrpc_abuse</td><td>URI contains xmlrpc.php</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Additional CVE patterns in <code>_G.config.vulnerability.cve_patterns</code> (init.lua): CVE-2024-25600 (Bricks Builder RCE), CVE-2024-27956 (WP Automatic SQLi), CVE-2024-1071 (Ultimate Member SQLi), CVE-2022-0739 (BookingPress SQLi), CVE-2024-2879 (LayerSlider SQLi).</p>
  <p><code>CVE-2024-8425</code> shares the same Gift Voucher upload pattern as <code>CVE-2025-47577</code> and is noted as an alias in code comments, but <code>vulnerability_rules.lua</code> only ever returns <code>CVE-2025-47577</code> for that match — it is not an independently-matched 14th CVE. The Suricata layer does detect it separately (see <a href="/docs/detection/suricata">Suricata Network IDS</a>, SID 1000010), since its signature matches on a distinct PHP-upload body pattern rather than the URI.</p>

  <h2 id="suricata">Suricata Alert-Based Detections</h2>
  <p>The second CVE path is network evidence: Suricata's CVE signatures (SIDs 1000001–1000016) raise the source
  IP's reputation by severity (1 → +50, 2 → +25, otherwise +10, capped at 100), and a flagged IP is diverted at
  routing stage 6. The reason string carries the signature, so a Suricata-confirmed IP maps to the
  <code>suricata_confirmed_alert</code> AbuseIPDB reason below. Parsing, private-IP filtering and decay are on
  <a href="/docs/detection/suricata#integration">Suricata Network IDS</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="abuseipdb">AbuseIPDB Reportability Mapping</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Honeypot Reason</th><th>Categories</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td>cve_pattern_match</td><td>15, 21</td><td>CVE pattern detected via URL/argument matching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>vulnerable_plugin_access</td><td>15, 21</td><td>Access on plugins known to have specific CVEs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>multiple_admin_attempts</td><td>18</td><td>Indicators of brute-force attempts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>rapid_automation_detected</td><td>19</td><td>Signatures of malicious bots</td></tr>
    <tr><td>suspicious_file_upload</td><td>21</td><td>File upload vector anomalies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>suricata_confirmed_alert</td><td>15, 21</td><td>An IP confirmed via Suricata alert</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Report Comment Format:</strong> <code>Honeypot detection: [Honeypot Reason] (CVEs: [List of Matched CVEs])</code></p>

  <h2 id="adding">Adding a CVE</h2>
  <p>Seeding a new vulnerability is deliberately boring. Pick the narrowest signature that proves the
  exploit attempt, then add it to both the Lua rules and the matching Suricata rule where possible.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Confirm the request shape.</strong> Record the vulnerable endpoint, the required argument or header, and a benign request to the same endpoint that must not match.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a Lua pattern.</strong> Extend <code>vulnerability_rules.lua</code> (or <code>cve_patterns</code> in <code>init.lua</code>) with the CVE identifier, severity, plugin, attack vector and description.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a Suricata signature.</strong> Allocate the next SID in the appropriate range and align it with the WSTG category.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a unit test.</strong> Cover a positive match, a negative request and the reported fields. Run the suite under plain Lua.</li>
    <li><strong>Update the table.</strong> Add the CVE to this page so operators know what the deployment recognises.</li>
    <li><strong>Regenerate the catalogue.</strong> Rebuild <code>patterns/patterns.json</code> so the new CVE appears in the dashboard and on the <a href="/docs/detection/patterns">Pattern Database</a> page without the two lists drifting apart.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>A rule in <code>vulnerability_rules.lua</code> returns the fields the reporters and the dashboard expect:</p>
  <pre><code>-- CVE-2023-2986: Abandoned Cart Lite Hardcoded Encryption Key
if string.find(combined, "wcal_action=checkout_link") and
   string.find(combined, "validate=") then
    return &#123;
        cve = "CVE-2023-2986",
        severity = "HIGH",
        description = "Abandoned Cart Lite exploitation using hardcoded encryption key",
        plugin = "abandoned-cart-lite",
        attack_vector = "encryption_bypass"
    &#125;
end</code></pre>
  <p>The quick-match table in <code>init.lua</code> takes one Lua pattern per CVE (escape <code>-</code> and <code>.</code>):</p>
  <pre><code>cve_patterns = &#123;
    ["CVE-2023-28121"] = "X%-WCPAY%-PLATFORM%-CHECKOUT%-USER",
    ["CVE-2023-2986"] = "wcal_action=checkout_link",
    ["CVE-2024-25600"] = "bricks/v1/render_element",
    -- ...</code></pre>
  <p>And the matching test in <code>tests/test_vulnerability_rules.lua</code>, with its negative case:</p>
  <pre><code>local cve2 = rules.detect_vulnerability_pattern("/", "wcal_action=checkout_link&amp;validate=x", nil)
check("CVE-2023-2986 detected", cve2 ~= nil and cve2.cve == "CVE-2023-2986")

local clean = rules.detect_vulnerability_pattern("/shop/product/red-shirt", nil, nil)
check("clean uri returns nil", clean == nil)</code></pre>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> 13 seeded CVEs across multiple exploit vectors, severity-graded Suricata integration, AbuseIPDB reportability for 6 reason types.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Coverage limited to seeded CVEs (manual addition required), Suricata same-host testing gap, generic patterns may need tuning for new WordPress versions.</p>
</DocsLayout>
